# Base-distance stage anchoring and room-consistent placement

Status: IN_PROGRESS
Milestone: Consistent character size across demo, recorded and imported sources
Dependencies: 12_PERSPECTIVE_LAB.todo, 20_CORE_MOTION_UPGRADE.todo, 22_IMPORTED_SCALE_FRAMING.todo

## Purpose
Presentation scale follows the measured body span directly, so the character is drawn as large
as the performer happens to appear in the source frame. The scripted demo fills the stage while
a performer standing back from a phone camera becomes small, and the imported GLB reads smaller
still because it estimates that span from median bone ratios rather than landmark extents.

Anchor the session to a base distance instead: the first fully visible body defines the size the
character is drawn at, and later observations scale by the ratio of spans. The same ratio yields
a room depth so the figure can stand on the floor plane rather than float in image space.

## Tasks
- [x] Measure the reported mismatch on clean renders and the supplied screenshots.
- [x] Trace the scale path through presentation, retargeting and the room projection.
- [x] Add a session-scoped base-distance anchor with capture, reset and depth mapping.
- [x] Cover capture, perspective scaling, clamping, reacquisition and source changes with tests.
- [ ] Drive imported-model presentation from the anchor instead of raw apparent span.
- [ ] Drive the built-in character from the same anchor so both paths agree.
- [ ] Place the character on the room floor plane at the derived depth.
- [ ] Expose Recalibrate and reset the anchor on source changes in Studio.
- [ ] Record native and recorded-source evidence, then update guides and the handbook.

## Acceptance criteria
- [x] The first fully visible body fixes the drawn size for the session.
- [x] Apparent size varying with distance scales the character by the inverse of depth.
- [x] Derived depth stays inside the room bounds and reports when it was clamped.
- [x] A partial body or an unanchored session never fabricates a placement.
- [x] The anchor is session-only and is never written to disk.
- [ ] Built-in and imported avatars are drawn at a comparable size on the same source.
- [ ] Stepping back moves the character up the floor grid rather than only shrinking it.

## Completion evidence
- Measured before state: demo built-in 71.0% of stage height, demo GLB 70.0%, the supplied
  reference-video capture 22.0% and the supplied imported-model capture 21.7%. The two demo
  screenshots the owner supplied as the target are byte-identical (SHA256 a6634d38...).
- Root cause: presentation.calibration_ratio returns median(measured image span / projected posed
  span) / max(frame_size), which presentation.present multiplies back by max(frame_size), so the
  drawn size equals the performer's apparent pixel size with no reference framing.
- src\trickroom\placement.py adds StageAnchor, StagePlacement and body_span.
- tests\test_stage_anchor.py: 31 focused cases covering capture, inverse-depth scaling, clamping,
  partial bodies, reacquisition, frame-size changes, explicit reset and invalid configuration.
- Full suite 405 tests, no failures, errors or skips; Ruff check and format pass.
- This is a presentation anchor derived from image spans. It is not a measured physical distance,
  a calibrated focal length, a floor-contact estimate or any claim about tracking accuracy.
- Renderer integration, room-floor placement and native evidence are not yet done.

## Completion log
- 2026-10-14 - Reported by the owner: demo large, recorded source small, imported model smaller.
  Reproduced and measured; anchoring module and focused regressions landed. Integration remains.
